cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Charting New Territory: Digital Humanities and AI in African
  Studies — workshop website
message: >-
  If you refer to this website or reuse its code or data,
  please cite it using the metadata below. To cite the
  workshop's position paper instead, see `references`.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Frédérick
    family-names: Madore
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0959-2092'
    affiliation: University of Bayreuth
  - given-names: Vincent
    family-names: Hiribarren
    affiliation: King's College London
repository-code: 'https://github.com/fmadore/dh-ai-african-studies-2026'
url: 'https://fmadore.github.io/dh-ai-african-studies-2026/'
abstract: >-
  Post-event archive for "Charting New Territory: Digital
  Humanities and AI in African Studies", a three-day
  international scoping workshop held 18–20 February 2026 at
  the Xplanatorium Herrenhausen in Hanover, Germany, and
  funded by the Volkswagen Foundation. The site documents the
  workshop's outcomes: a photo gallery, participant
  interviews, an interactive concept map, a participant
  directory and map, and a faceted bibliography.
keywords:
  - digital humanities
  - artificial intelligence
  - African studies
  - workshop
  - Volkswagen Foundation
# Two licences, matching README.md and src/lib/data/site-meta.ts: MIT for the
# source code, CC BY-NC 4.0 for the site's text and compiled data. Photographs
# and video interviews are excluded from both — they stay with their author.
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-NC-4.0
version: 2.0.0
date-released: '2026-08-05'
references:
  # The workshop's principal output. Bibliographic source of truth is
  # src/lib/data/position-paper-meta.ts — keep this entry in step with it.
  # Author order is load-bearing: the two conveners, then everyone else
  # alphabetically. It implies no hierarchy of contribution.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      For Whom and For What Purpose? A Position Paper on Digital
      Humanities and AI in African Studies
    status: submitted
    year: 2026
    month: 9
    journal: ZMO Programmatic Texts
    issn: '2191-3242'
    publisher:
      name: Leibniz-Zentrum Moderner Orient (ZMO)
    license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
    languages:
      - eng
    abstract: >-
      Synthesises the outcomes of the workshop into a set of
      recommendations for researchers, funders, and institutions
      working at the intersection of digital humanities, artificial
      intelligence, and African studies. It centres African
      perspectives and addresses infrastructure gaps, linguistic
      diversity, equity, methodology, and ethics.
    keywords:
      - digital humanities
      - artificial intelligence
      - African studies
      - open science
      - research infrastructure
      - multilingualism
      - data governance
      - ethics
      - policy
